    "rationale": "Evidence: iteration 5 is the best raced design (score 137.76): body 0.18 x 0.12 m, 0.8 kg, five 16 mm sensors at y 0.06 m with x = -0.06 to +0.06 m, wheel_track 0.12 m, kp 3.0, ki 0.3, kd 0.4, base_speed 1.8, last_direction. Every raced trial ends off track at max_error about 0.061 to 0.063 m, while rms_error stays flat at about 0.03 m across tracks and iterations. That pattern points to a steady offset rather than a gain-limited overshoot.

What I accepted from the round-1 critique: (1) The draft's kp 5.0 is a large untested jump. At base 1.8 the base PWM is about 3686, and a signal of about 2 with kp 5 gives turn about 10 (3500 PWM), so the wheels saturate near the edges. I therefore use kp 4.0, which keeps a stiffer correction but stops short of the largest step. (2) The critique is right that the draft's expected-result language has no magnitude and that kd was not raised to match a stiffer loop. I raise kd modestly from 0.4 to 0.5 so the damping-to-stiffness ratio does not fall as far. (3) The critique is right that the draft's geometry-and-gain changes cannot be attributed, so I keep the iteration-5 geometry exactly, which is the best-raced geometry. (4) I correct my own earlier claim: the 'ki 0 vs 1.0 identical' comparison was never raced, because the ki 1.0 design was a draft that was not run. Only ki 0.3 and ki 0.6 have race evidence, and ki 0.6 scored lower (122.2). I therefore keep ki 0.3 and do not test ki 1.0 here.

What I rejected: kp 5.0 (saturation risk, see above), kd 1.0 (iteration 4 gave no measured oscillation gain from kd), base_speed 1.3 or 1.1 (the 1.88 s oval finish and the 137.8 vs 132.0 gain from base 1.6 to 1.8 favour keeping 1.8), and the draft's geometry change to 0.14 x 0.10 m with sensors at y 0.07 (mass, body and sensor lead changed together, so the result would be unattributable).

